				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$J^\pi = 3{\mathrm{/}}2^ + ,\ell = 0$$\end{document}</tex-math><mml:math id="M52"><mml:msup><mml:mrow><mml:mi>J</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mi>&#x003c0;</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:msup><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">&#x02215;</mml:mi><mml:msup><mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mo>+</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:msup><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>&#x02113;</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>0</mml:mn></mml:math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41467_2018_8052_Article_IEq25.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> contribution are substantial only in the proximity of <inline-formula id="IEq26"><alternatives><tex-math id="M53">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\theta _{{\mathrm{c}}.{\mathrm{m}}.} = 180^\circ$$\end{document}</tex-math><mml:math id="M54"><mml:msub><mml:mrow><mml:mi>&#x003b8;</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">c</mml:mi><mml:mo>.</mml:mo><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">m</mml:mi><mml:mo>.</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>18</mml:mn><mml:msup><mml:mrow><mml:mn>0</mml:mn></mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mo>&#x02218;</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:msup></mml:math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41467_2018_8052_Article_IEq26.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> (see, for example, Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig3" ref-type="fig">3b</xref>), and hence have only a minor effect on angle-averaged observables, such as the reaction cross section. We note that <italic>&#x003b4;</italic> is nearly independent of the value of <italic>p</italic><sub>zz</sub>, indicating that the analyzing power of the deuterium does not play any role in the enhancement of the cross section (a consequence of the nearly-zero value of the integral of <inline-formula id="IEq27"><alternatives><tex-math id="M55">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$A_{{\mathrm{zz}}}^{({\mathrm{b}})}$$\end{document}</tex-math><mml:math id="M56"><mml:msubsup><mml:mrow><mml:mi>A</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">zz</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mo>(</mml:mo><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">b</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mo>)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:mrow></mml:msubsup></mml:math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41467_2018_8052_Article_IEq27.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>). However, the factor <italic>&#x003b4;</italic> varies as a function of the energy and drops significantly above the deuteron incident energy of 0.8&#x02009;MeV. This is shown in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig4" ref-type="fig">4b</xref> for the maximum enhancement (which is found for <inline-formula id="IEq28"><alternatives><tex-math id="M57">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
